{"schema_version":1,"id":"018f0000-0000-7000-8000-00000000c3b1","stream":{"kind":"session","id":"01a0c930-3251-7961-8bb5-544ec0b1a36c"},"sequence":51,"recorded_at":1780531400000097,"record_type":"event","durability":"durable","causation_id":"54c40bac-01d0-401d-8b14-649734ac40cf","payload_type":"tool.result","payload_schema_version":1,"payload":{"kind":"tool_result","command_id":"54c40bac-01d0-401d-8b14-649734ac40cf","run_stream":{"kind":"run","id":"54c40bac-01d0-401d-8b14-649734ac40cf"},"call_id":"call_01a0c9306ed7752c920abaa45a9b5615","text":"Read text file `/Users/iv/Developer/ReluxWorks/agent-session-manager/.temp/resources/TASK-260830-1c28dz/TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev8.md`.\n1|THIS BRIEF SUPERSEDES TASK-260830-1c28dz_rework-rev7.md and every earlier precondition. Do exactly what is written here.\n2|Rework run for TASK-260830-1c28dz. Revision 7 was reviewed by RUN-260922-da502e (codex gpt-6-astra low) and routed CHANGES REQUESTED: two P1 and one P2. Read TASK-260830-1c28dz_review-verdict-rev7.md first.\n3|\n4|**I HAVE NARROWED THIS LEAF'S SCOPE. Read this before anything else.** Revision 7's P1-B — the §4.C overlap capability gates (`multi_attach` for overlapping clients, `multiple_input_clients` plus AX policy for concurrent input) — is **no longer yours**. It needs an authoritative active-client/liveness contract that does not exist anywhere in the tree, and building that inside a leaf already on its seventh revision is the wrong shape. It is now **TASK-260922-vcx6yo (active-client-census-and-overlap-admission)** in this same Story, carrying both reviewer scenarios as its acceptance criteria. Your obligation for it is exactly two things: **fail closed where overlap cannot be established**, and record the bound in the census and TRACEABILITY naming TASK-260922-vcx6yo as its owner. Do not implement a liveness subsystem. Do not silently claim the §4.C attach row complete.\n5|\n6|**And one correction about revision 7's results.** They state that the sibling paths have no admission-to-effect interval \"because the outer loop rechecks by construction\". The reviewer disproved that with a counterexample on the stop path. I asked in the last brief whether the same interval existed on create, restore and stop; asserting that it does not is not the same as measuring it. From here, a claim of that form must come with the probe that would have found the counterexample.\n7|\n8|1. **P1-A — stop escalation executes after its authorization facts go stale.** `Execute(request-stop) → executeEngineOp → terminstance.Engine → backend.PerformEffect(process_closed) → confirmClosed`: `backend.go:356-379` checks live authorization and generation in the engine **before** `pollSession`, and after that wait `confirmClosed` issues `kill-session` at line 369 **without revalidating generation, authorization expiry, current lease or the operation deadline**. The next engine recheck refuses only after the kill has already happened. `TestReviewStopRechecksBeforeEscalation/{generation,authorization,deadline}` drives the production entry, sends the graceful interrupt, changes exactly one admission fact during the `has-session` poll — generation rotated to `generation-two`, authorization expiring at +3 ms, the independent request deadline at +3 ms, with the fake clock advancing 10 ms past the 5 ms graceful wait — and observes `kill-session` anyway. Each case fails twice under `-race` with no data-race diagnostic. The returned result already lists `graceful_stop_requested` and `process_closed`; the eventual stale-generation, unauthorized or stop-timeout error does not undo a kill that happened. B42 covers attach's immediate post-check window, not a poll followed by a new destructive command, and B29's unmeasured poll boundary is not an authorization deferral. Carry the revalidation contract to the **actual command boundary after every wait, including escalation**, and distinguish a graceful timeout from the overall operation deadline. Commit a negative that asserts **no `kill-session` command was issued**, not merely the final error. Then audit every other multi-command or waiting effect with the same invariant and either close it or state a precise owner-bound limitation — with the probe, per the correction above.\n9|2. **P2 — an attach deadline rejects after waiting but does not cancel the wait.** `ops.go:102` calls `ServerAdmission()` synchronously with no deadline or context contract, and `ops.go:136` takes an uncancellable mutex; the new deadline check at line 141 runs only after both waits finish. It correctly prevents the late receipt — keep that — but a blocked admission or another barrier holder can block the request indefinitely past its deadline. With a 200 ms operation deadline and hour-long authorization, each call stays blocked until the reviewer releases it at ~401-405 ms. An expired **and context-cancelled** attach also stays blocked until external unlock. This is §4.C's \"a deadline cancels waiting, not a committed effect\", whose waiting half is still open. Bound the waits themselves, assert no late receipt or background commit after the caller has timed out, and apply the waiting contract consistently to every barrier user.\n10|3. **What this revision must contain to be acceptable.** Items 1 and 2 closed by construction, with the audit of the other waiting and multi-command effects backed by probes; the overlap bound recorded against TASK-260922-vcx6yo with a fail-closed refusal where overlap cannot be established; the census and TRACEABILITY updated for both; and the results' sibling-path claim replaced by measurement. If after that work items 1 or 2 turn out to need a change in a landed owner outside `internal/tmuxserver`, name the owner and the contract and hand off the rest — that remains an acceptable revision, and it is better than an eighth round of local guards.\n11|\n12|PROCESS. Keep the candidate UNCOMMITTED; exactly one leaf's worth of delta on top of the checkpoint; base `d4bd91d0`, and if trunk moves refresh through the managed path and repeat the path-by-path audit. Never commit on the Story branch, never edit `.task-board` directly, never self-accept, and do NOT touch `internal/traceability` — the final leaf g0pcnt carries the registry. Keep the zero-real-tmux determinism property. Run your own package tests, the full importer closure, `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...`, and the harness BEFORE handoff, then the full configured suite once from `spawn.worktree_isolation.validation.commands`. Update the results with a finding-by-finding table, the effect-boundary and waiting audits with their probes, and the overlap bound with its owner; update the conformance matrix and the evidence archive, attach the results standalone, verify every attachment by reading it back off the board, then `task-board handoff TASK-260830-1c28dz --role developer` so revision 8 is constructed, and exit promptly. Hand off no later than 3 hours in. Canonical CLI /Users/iv/.curator/global/bin/task-board. Model: muse-spark max. The independent reviewer will be codex gpt-6-astra low.\n13|REJECTION PATTERNS THE INDEPENDENT REVIEWER HAS ALREADY CAUGHT ON THIS EPIC (2026-09-17: TASK-260830-1geqhj rev1, TASK-260830-24z2b3 rev2, TASK-260830-219okr rev3 were all routed CHANGES REQUESTED, and between them these six shapes account for every finding). Read this as a checklist you run against your own work BEFORE handoff; each item names what a reviewer will do to your candidate.\n14|(a) Circular expectations. An assertion that compares a production value against a production constant (`got.Code == pkg.SomeUnsupportedCode`) pins nothing: swapping the constant to another registered value with the same exit keeps the suite green. Assert the LITERAL token from the pinned specification text, at the production entry, and make sure the literal actually occurs in a committed test — not only in a comment. Derive every expectation from the spec, never from the implementation.\n15|(b) A rule pinned at the helper instead of the entry. A test that calls the inner helper directly proves the helper; it says nothing about whether the composed production entry still reaches it. Drive every rule through the entry point the conformance matrix names as its call site, and if a rule is implemented TWICE (a Build path and a Decode path, an exported entry and its internal sibling), each side needs its own narrowing mutant — a rule pinned only on one side leaves the other side unmeasured, and reviewers plant exactly there.\n16|(c) Forking a landed gate. Never re-implement what internal/environ, internal/canonicaljson, internal/scalar or another landed owner already gates (string bounds in CHARACTERS not bytes, uint53, digests, UUIDv7, timestamps, sorted-unique arrays, closed-shape decoding, the AX number model that forbids floating point, values at or beyond 2^53 and rounding-and-continuing). Delegate to it and keep only your package-specific rules. Every fork this epic produced became a defect: a byte-measured bound, an extension value rounded at 2^60, nested duplicate members collapsed by a lenient decode.\n17|(d) A census or an inventory standing in for behaviour. A reflection census of field NAMES does not prove that a cached observation cannot authorize; an arm inventory keyed by message text does not prove a condition edit is caught. Drive the input, assert the effect.\n18|(e) Mutant rows that do not measure what they claim. An arm-delete (`&& false`) is not a narrowing; a row patching two sites whose kill comes from one is one measured site reported as two; a plant with no raw log and no subprocess exit is a verdict line, not evidence. Ship one raw log per plant per run, state narrowing vs arm-delete per row, and include an applied harmless SURVIVED control so the harness is proven able to report a survivor.\n19|(f) Prose that outruns the tests. \"Composes X\", \"never admits what decoding refuses\", \"nothing re-implemented\", \"no cached sentinel can authorize\" are censusable claims: a reviewer greps for the callers and plants the counter-example. Before you write a sentence like that in a doc comment, README, LOGBOOK or TRACEABILITY row, make a test fail without the property. If you cannot, state it as an explicit BOUND instead, naming what is not covered.\n20|Finally: report the coverage ratio you MEASURED, not the one you planned. Two of the three rejected revisions claimed a complete ratio and the reviewer measured a smaller one; an honest smaller number with named gaps is accepted, an inflated one is a finding.","correlation_facts":{"tool_name":"read_file","outcome":"success"}}}
